1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:02,026
The Science Behind Cannabis.

2
00:00:02,026 --> 00:00:05,079
Episode five: How the endocannabinoid system works.

3
00:00:05,079 --> 00:00:10,346
We now have all the components: two receptors, two ligands, and the enzymes around them.

4
00:00:10,346 --> 00:00:14,177
In this episode we see how they operate together at the synapse.

5
00:00:15,677 --> 00:00:18,251
The endocannabinoid system has three parts.

6
00:00:18,251 --> 00:00:22,563
The receptors, CB1 and CB2, sit in cell membranes and detect the signal.

7
00:00:22,563 --> 00:00:25,916
The ligands, anandamide and 2-AG, are the signal itself.

8
00:00:25,916 --> 00:00:28,850
And a set of enzymes controls the signal in time:

9
00:00:28,850 --> 00:00:34,359
some enzymes build the ligands when they are needed, and others destroy them a moment later.

10
00:00:34,359 --> 00:00:37,891
Because the ligands are made on demand and removed quickly,

11
00:00:37,891 --> 00:00:43,340
the whole system is designed for brief, local messages rather than long-lasting broadcasts.

12
00:00:43,340 --> 00:00:47,951
Understanding the enzymes is therefore essential to understanding the system.

13
00:00:49,451 --> 00:00:52,831
Both endocannabinoids are cut out of membrane lipids.

14
00:00:52,831 --> 00:01:01,122
Anandamide is produced from a phospholipid precursor called N-acyl-phosphatidylethanolamine (NAPE), mainly by the enzyme NAPE-PLD,

15
00:01:01,122 --> 00:01:03,800
although several alternative routes exist.

16
00:01:03,800 --> 00:01:06,351
Two A G is produced in two steps: first,

17
00:01:06,351 --> 00:01:14,259
the enzyme phospholipase C-beta (PLCβ) generates diacylglycerol (DAG), and then diacylglycerol lipase (DAGL), releases 2-AG.

18
00:01:14,259 --> 00:01:18,532
These enzymes are switched on by a rise in calcium inside the cell,

19
00:01:18,532 --> 00:01:21,338
or by activation of certain other receptors.

20
00:01:21,338 --> 00:01:27,078
That is what makes the system responsive: the ligand appears only when the cell is active.

21
00:01:28,578 --> 00:01:30,154
Signals must also end.

22
00:01:30,154 --> 00:01:35,027
Anandamide is destroyed mainly by fatty acid amide hydrolase (FAAH),

23
00:01:35,027 --> 00:01:39,111
an enzyme identified by Benjamin Cravatt's group in 1996;

24
00:01:39,111 --> 00:01:43,410
it splits anandamide into arachidonic acid and ethanolamine.

25
00:01:43,410 --> 00:01:49,501
Two A G is destroyed mainly by monoacylglycerol lipase (MAGL), characterised in 2002,

26
00:01:49,501 --> 00:01:53,156
which splits it into arachidonic acid and glycerol.

27
00:01:53,156 --> 00:01:59,461
Other enzymes, including ABHD6, ABHD12 and cyclooxygenase-2 (COX-2), contribute as well.

28
00:01:59,461 --> 00:02:05,409
Because degradation is fast, an endocannabinoid signal normally lasts only seconds,

29
00:02:05,409 --> 00:02:08,777
and stays close to the place where it was made.

30
00:02:10,277 --> 00:02:12,730
Where in the synapse does all this happen?

31
00:02:12,730 --> 00:02:16,820
The answer came in 2001 from three laboratories working independently:

32
00:02:16,820 --> 00:02:22,428
Rachel Wilson and Roger Nicoll in San Francisco, Takako Ohno-Shosaku and Masanobu Kano in Japan,

33
00:02:22,428 --> 00:02:25,116
and Anatol Kreitzer and Wade Regehr in Boston.

34
00:02:25,116 --> 00:02:28,913
Each found that when a postsynaptic neuron is strongly activated,

35
00:02:28,913 --> 00:02:31,542
it briefly suppresses the inputs it receives.

36
00:02:31,542 --> 00:02:37,443
The messenger responsible was an endocannabinoid, and the direction was the reverse of the usual one:

37
00:02:37,443 --> 00:02:41,766
the signal travelled from the receiving neuron back to the sending neuron.

38
00:02:41,766 --> 00:02:43,928
This is called retrograde signalling.

39
00:02:45,428 --> 00:02:46,717
Here is the sequence.

40
00:02:46,717 --> 00:02:50,769
First, the postsynaptic neuron is activated and calcium enters it.

41
00:02:50,769 --> 00:02:56,724
Second, the calcium switches on the synthesising enzymes, and 2-AG is produced from the membrane.

42
00:02:56,724 --> 00:02:59,917
Third, 2-AG moves backwards across the synaptic gap.

43
00:02:59,917 --> 00:03:03,539
Fourth, it binds CB1 receptors on the presynaptic terminal.

44
00:03:03,539 --> 00:03:09,924
Fifth, CB1, through its G protein, closes calcium channels and opens potassium channels in the terminal.

45
00:03:09,924 --> 00:03:14,775
Sixth, with less calcium entering, the terminal releases less neurotransmitter.

46
00:03:14,775 --> 00:03:20,116
If the terminal releases GABA, the effect is called DSI; if it releases glutamate, DSE.

47
00:03:20,116 --> 00:03:23,984
In both cases, the receiving cell has tuned down its own input.

48
00:03:25,484 --> 00:03:29,937
Retrograde signalling makes the endocannabinoid system a feedback mechanism:

49
00:03:29,937 --> 00:03:33,921
it lets a neuron adjust the strength of the connections it receives,

50
00:03:33,921 --> 00:03:35,679
according to its own activity.

51
00:03:35,679 --> 00:03:42,007
The adjustment can be brief, lasting seconds, or it can trigger longer-lasting changes in synaptic strength,

52
00:03:42,007 --> 00:03:44,878
a form of plasticity called long-term depression.

53
00:03:44,878 --> 00:03:51,030
Because CB1 receptors sit on both inhibitory and excitatory terminals throughout the cortex, hippocampus,

54
00:03:51,030 --> 00:03:55,717
basal ganglia and cerebellum, this feedback operates across many brain circuits.

55
00:03:55,717 --> 00:04:01,401
Outside the brain, CB2 on immune cells responds to endocannabinoids as part of immune signalling.

56
00:04:02,901 --> 00:04:07,670
Mechanistically, the plant molecules enter this picture in different ways.

57
00:04:07,670 --> 00:04:10,249
THC is a partial agonist at CB1 and CB2:

58
00:04:10,249 --> 00:04:14,954
it occupies the same receptors as 2-AG and anandamide and activates them,

59
00:04:14,954 --> 00:04:17,403
though not to the same degree as 2-AG.

60
00:04:17,403 --> 00:04:20,175
The main difference is timing and location.

61
00:04:20,175 --> 00:04:25,460
An endocannabinoid is made at one synapse, acts there, and is gone within seconds.

62
00:04:25,460 --> 00:04:31,455
THC arrives through the bloodstream, is not produced or removed by the synapse's own enzymes,

63
00:04:31,455 --> 00:04:36,225
and therefore reaches CB1 receptors across many circuits at the same time.

64
00:04:36,225 --> 00:04:41,897
CBD, in contrast, binds CB1 only weakly, and its reported actions involve other targets,

65
00:04:41,897 --> 00:04:45,313
among them the TRPV1 ion channel and the enzyme FAAH.

66
00:04:46,813 --> 00:04:48,760
The picture continues to grow.

67
00:04:48,760 --> 00:04:50,836
Anandamide also activates TRPV1,

68
00:04:50,836 --> 00:04:55,702
the ion channel that responds to heat and to capsaicin from chilli peppers.

69
00:04:55,702 --> 00:05:01,477
Other proteins, such as the orphan receptor GPR55 and the nuclear receptors called PPARs,

70
00:05:01,477 --> 00:05:03,877
respond to some cannabinoids as well.

71
00:05:03,877 --> 00:05:09,652
In 1998, Ben-Shabat and Mechoulam showed that related lipids released together with 2-AG,

72
00:05:09,652 --> 00:05:14,648
which do not bind the receptor themselves, can increase the activity of 2-AG,

73
00:05:14,648 --> 00:05:17,048
and coined the term entourage effect.

74
00:05:17,048 --> 00:05:21,655
The endocannabinoid system, first revealed by the chemistry of a plant,

75
00:05:21,655 --> 00:05:26,911
is now understood as a widespread lipid signalling system of the vertebrate body,

76
00:05:26,911 --> 00:05:28,792
and research on it continues.

77
00:05:30,292 --> 00:05:36,423
This concludes The Science Behind Cannabis: from the isolation of THC in 1964, to the receptors,

78
00:05:36,423 --> 00:05:42,362
the body's own ligands, and the enzymes and synapses that make up the endocannabinoid system.

79
00:05:42,362 --> 00:05:43,831
Thank you for watching.
